// A ticking clock is a library value, not a language construct (Decisions §9): there is no `clock`
// declaration, no `live` binding, and no `every` clause. `@tao/time` exposes a ticker whose reading
// changes over time, and ordinary `let` derives from it.
// `Value` is the time as of the latest tick, `Running` whether it ticks, and `Start()` and `Stop()`
// control it. A ticker starts when the view holding it mounts and stops when that view unmounts;
// `Start()` on a running ticker and `Stop()` on a stopped one do nothing.
public
type Ticker is {
   Value time
   Running boolean
   Start action()
   Stop action()
}

// `Interval(Every)` constructs a ticker that ticks every `Every`. The lowering is the obvious one —
// an interval timer plus a state update per tick — reached through the expression-position `from`
// mechanism any package uses, so the language carries no timer grammar at all.
public
function Interval(Every duration) returns Ticker {
   return Interval(Every) from ./Time.ts
}
